WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.823
The ferret shows that a progenitor's menu changes with time.

2
00:00:07.623 --> 00:00:09.563
But what actually changes inside the cell?

3
00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:12.593
The fly asks it in the cleanest form.

4
00:00:13.473 --> 00:00:16.873
Each half-segment of its nerve cord has about 30 neuroblasts.

5
00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:21.957
Each neuroblast buds off ganglion mother cells, one after another.

6
00:00:22.897 --> 00:00:25.277
Each of those divides into neurons or glia.

7
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:32.510
In 2001, Takako Isshiki, Bret Pearson, Scott Holbrook and Chris Doe found a clock of

8
00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:33.530
transcription factors.

9
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.480
Most neuroblasts switch on four factors in turn.

10
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.240
Hunchback, then Krüppel, then Pdm, then Castor.

11
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.240
A ganglion mother cell keeps the factor that was on when it budded off.

12
00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.020
So do its neurons.

13
00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:54.883
The windows overlap, so the sequence is an outline, not a set of sharp steps.

14
00:00:55.917 --> 00:00:57.797
One lineage makes this concrete.

15
00:00:58.537 --> 00:01:04.617
The neuroblast NB7-1 makes its first five motor neurons, U1 to U5, in that order.

16
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:11.270
In this idealized picture, U1 and U2 come in the Hunchback window, and U3 with

17
00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:14.350
Krüppel. U4 comes with Pdm alone.

18
00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.750
U5 needs Pdm and Castor together.

19
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:22.517
Now read a grid. Rows are place, which neuroblast.

20
00:01:23.577 --> 00:01:24.857
Columns are time, which window.

21
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.180
The same factor gives a different neuron in a different row.

22
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.080
But expression in sequence is only a correlation.

23
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.060
To show the factors set identity, take one away, or keep it on too long.

24
00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.230
Hunchback and Krüppel also pattern the early embryo.

25
00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:45.670
So the tests changed them in the nervous system itself.

26
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:51.350
Without Hunchback in the nervous system, first-born identities were lost in the

27
00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:52.350
lineages examined.

28
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.360
Predict. Keep Hunchback on long after it would switch off.

29
00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.640
Do the extra cells look early-born or late-born?

30
00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:02.460
And of which lineage?

31
00:02:08.483 --> 00:02:11.623
The lineages made extra cells with first-born features.

32
00:02:12.603 --> 00:02:16.363
In NB7-1, extra cells resembled U1 or U2.

33
00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:19.667
Each lineage made its own first-born type.

34
00:02:20.547 --> 00:02:23.127
Hunchback did not make every first-born cell the same.

35
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.100
Prolonged Krüppel made extra second-born cells.

36
00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:36.350
In two thousand six, the same laboratory showed that P D M and Castor specify U four

37
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:37.350
and U five.

38
00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:42.750
Birth order had become a genetic program that could be broken and rewired.

39
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.860
The takeaway: place picks the class of neuron.

40
00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:51.220
Time, read as a cascade of factors, picks the member within it.
